        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Liangzhu culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> Delta and the Taihu Lake area of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>. Key sites: Liangzhu Ancient City and its Mojiaoshan palace complex in Yuhang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>; the Yaoshan altar; Maqiao, Xindili, Miaoqian and Guangfulin.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 3300–2300 BCE; it flourished around 2500 BCE and disappeared from the Taihu Lake area after about 2300 BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">jade cong cylinders, bi discs and yue axes; the walled Liangzhu Ancient City; flood-control dams; paddy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, irrigation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquaculture">aquaculture</a>; houses on stilts; corundum axes worked with diamond tools; black “eggshell” pottery; the Yaoshan altar</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations since 1936, including the city wall bases announced in 2007; graded burials and grave goods; analysis of jade, tools and pottery; flood layers and climate records; ancient DNA from Maqiao and Xindili and a 2025 autosomal study.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">7 min read · Last reviewed October 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Liangzhu culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="Yangtze River" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Yangtze River <span class="kid-tale-cr">Andrew Hitchcock, CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 5,000 years ago, the Liangzhu people lived by the Yangtze River in eastern China.</p><p>Their land was full of rivers, so many houses stood on stilts, safe from floods.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rice.jpg" alt="Rice" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Rice <span class="kid-tale-cr">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>They grew rice in wet fields, brought water to their crops and farmed fish.</p><p>Their biggest city had clay walls with six gates, and probably dams against floods.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silk.jpg" alt="Silk" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Silk <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kuebi = Armin Kübelbeck, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Rich people were buried with jade, silk and ivory, but poorer people mostly with pots.</p><p>Jade carvers made tubes called cong and flat discs called bi.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>About 4,300 years ago, heavy rain and floods probably drove them from their land.</p><p>Their jade shaped later Chinese art, and their city’s ruins are now protected by UNESCO.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Liangzhu was a highly stratified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-farming society of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> Delta whose jade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and lacquer went only to elite graves, a division that marks it as an early state.</li><li>Its centre, Liangzhu Ancient City near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, enclosed 290 hectares with clay walls and six gates around a palace complex, with dams to the north, and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</li><li>Its jade cong and bi set the standard for ritual jades across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the culture vanished from the Taihu Lake area after about 2300 BCE, probably because of floods.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Liangzhu craftsmen used diamond tools to polish corundum axes to a mirror-like lustre, the earliest known use of diamond tools anywhere, and Liangzhu is the only prehistoric culture known to have worked sapphire.</p></aside>
    </section>

